WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.029
Film Courage: What’s the secret to finding
truth when making a documentary?

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:07.939
I mean…is there anything that you say or
do that helps you find it?

00:00:07.939 --> 00:00:13.250
Joe Berlinger: We could spend hours and hours
and talk about truth in documentaries because

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:19.900
I…look…I’m a firm believer that all
documentary filmmaking, all media is inherently

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.930
subjective.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.080
You shoot hundreds of hours of footage.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.420
Only a couple of hours end up in your final
film.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.340
There are days you are not there to cover
a story.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.779
You have to choose a camera angle.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:37.050
Every camera angle communicates a different
emotion potentially.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.760
You make thousands of editorial decisions.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.731
So what’s guided me is that I’m not looking
for the capital truth (capital “T” truth

00:00:45.731 --> 00:00:52.040
in a situation) because I think the truth
is very elusive and subjective depending on

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.070
from what point of view you are looking at.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.880
What I look for in my films in as emotional
truthfulness.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.940
An emotional truthfulness about the human
condition or the subject that you’re looking

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:04.940
at.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.020
You know, in PARADISE LOST it because clear
that these guys were railroaded.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.620
But a different filmmaker possibly would have
made a different film.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:23.430
So I think filmmakers who think they are presenting
the absolute truth about a particular subject,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:33.270
I have potentially…certain kinds of films,
straightforward, history you know I think

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:42.700
the more you know hitting the truth whatever
that means is possible.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.750
But what I’m looking for is the emotional
truthfulness of a situation.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.020
The truth rises to the top if you allow both
sides to have their say.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:57.310
Treat the audience like a jury and not pound
a specific message over people’s heads.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.710
It has always been the method that I’ve
preferred.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:06.650
Even in this film, even though it’s a film
about denial, there’s a section of the film

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:10.490
in which those who don’t believe that it
took place, they’ve been given a chance

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:17.240
to air their views and we allow the audience
to make up their own minds about it.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.360
So to me that’s what I mean about emotional
truthfulness.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.230
You air all sides of an issue.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.319
Don’t pound a message over the viewers head
and you let the truth rise to the top.

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:36.080
In CRUDE, some people were surprised that
I allowed the executives to be interviewed

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.890
and to present their point of view about the
case.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:44.690
But I think I trust the audience to come to
the right conclusion about the emotional truthfulness

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.690
of the situation.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:50.280
And sometimes the emotional truthfulness of
a situation is not necessarily what you think

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.630
the film is about.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:59.069
You know for example, in CRUDE the indigenous
rainforest dwellers of Ecuador and their American

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:07.490
representatives were making a case and confronting
this major gasoline corporation for creating

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:11.319
this cancer zone the size of Rhode Island
through bad drilling practices.

00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:17.069
This company was claiming that this was not
the case and the pollution that is there is

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:20.440
the responsibility of the state oil company.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:25.360
Some people may think that I was making a
film about the absolute truth of that situation.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.879
For me, the absolute truth of the situation
(the emotional truth of the situation) that

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:37.830
I was trying to talk about is that we are
destroying the rainforest, that the people

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:46.390
who most deserve to be treated well were being
treated horribly.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:52.160
And that there were a whole other set of reasons
I was making that film, to bring justice to

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.670
these people.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:04:01.120
So sometimes the subjective truth of a situation
is not always what the filmmaker is necessarily

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.350
trying to present, at least that’s how I
look at it.

